<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Illyria</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Illyria was the name given by ancient Greek and Roman writers to the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a> along the eastern Adriatic, home of the Illyrian peoples. Its kingdoms, notably that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bardylis">Bardylis</a> and that of the Ardiaean dynasty under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agron">Agron</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teuta">Teuta</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gentius">Gentius</a>, fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> until the Roman conquest of 168 BCE. The region became the Roman province of Illyricum, and the name was revived for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illyrian-provinces">Illyrian Provinces</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/illyria.jpg" alt="Illyria" width="320" height="291" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ahmet Q. · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Illyrians_in_the_1st-2nd_centuries_CE.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
